WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.554
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.554 --> 00:00:05.130
I helped some toy makers
with their boot times.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.162
Will performance be the hot
toy this holiday season?

00:00:08.162 --> 00:00:09.120
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.512
Capital Fun is an
IoT toy developer,

00:00:15.512 --> 00:00:17.970
who has been converting over
portions of their architecture

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.960
to serverless systems.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.470
However, as their Google
Cloud Functions usage

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.080
didn't scale to meet demand
as fast as they wanted,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.025
they quickly pinned cold
boot time as the culprit

00:00:28.025 --> 00:00:30.150
and reached out to us to
see how they could improve

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.049
it, which totally makes sense.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.867
Even though GCF is considered
a serverless architecture,

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.200
you still have to be aware
that there are some cold boot

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.830
time overhead as new
instances are spun up.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.492
Now, most of the
time, your function

00:00:42.492 --> 00:00:43.950
will already be
running an instance

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.020
and will be ready to
handle future triggers

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.650
as fast as possible.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.040
However, your functions
might be in a cold state

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.860
because, number one, your
function has been deployed

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.200
but not triggered yet, or
number two, your function

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.350
has been idle long enough that
it was recycled for resources,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.090
or number three,
your function is

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.570
auto-scaling to handle capacity
and creating a new instance.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.690
In any of these cases, your
code isn't currently running,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.820
and the next trigger event
will spin up a new instance.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.020
Now, when it comes to GCF,
the number one contributor

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.590
to slow cold boot
performance is dependencies.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.310
When a module boots, node.js
resolves all the require calls

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.220
from the entry point using
synchronous I/O, which

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.990
can take a significant
amount of time

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.030
if the number of
dependencies are large

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.090
or if the content itself
requires a lot of linking.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.620
So when I saw that their GCF
module had 50 dependencies,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.810
well, we knew where
to focus our efforts.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.030
As a first step, we worked
with their tech team

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.700
to trim out any dependencies
that might not be needed.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.200
This includes areas
where they might

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.560
be using one function
from a library,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.610
but including the
entire library.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.760
So after a lot of negotiation,
digging, and spaghetti code

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.620
clinging, we were
able to get down

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.681
to eight packages, resulting in
a 2x performance in cold boot

00:01:56.681 --> 00:01:57.180
time.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.000
Which was good, but there
was still work to do.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.460
Next up, we looked at
what dependency versions

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.110
their functions
were actually using.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.450
More popular
versions of a package

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.750
are most likely already stored
in GCF's dependency cache,

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.250
meaning that importing
and resolving

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.690
can be done much quicker.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.330
So once we unified all of
Capital Fun's dependencies

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.220
to use the same
versions that were more

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.250
likely to be used in the cache,
we saw another nice performance

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:20.970
boost.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.860
Finally, digging through
the node.js code,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.400
we decided to set up lazy
loading of core requirements

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.910
rather than having them all
occur in cold boot phase.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.430
This allowed us to require only
what's needed at the start,

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.280
and then switch to an async
version of the required keyword

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.770
later for specific
request handling,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.140
bringing down the cold
boot times even further.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.350
With these small changes,
we saw a 10x improvement

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.600
in Capital Fun's
cold boot time, which

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.409
isn't bad for a day's work.

00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:49.200
If you want to know
more about the changes,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.340
check out the article in
the description below.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.464
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:54.690
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.260
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.690
every millisecond counts.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

